{"event_type":"tool_call","timestamp":"2026-06-19T13:48:53.319748Z","session_id":"2026-05-15_155415","project_id":"opencode_cc_mem","source":"magnolia-run","tool":"shell","args_summary":"bash -c # Read the full file via cat, but sed the Introduction section\npython3 << 'PYEOF'\nimport re\n\nwith open('/home/tjiang/repos/project_magnolia/opencode_cc_mem/projects/communication/paper/draft_v1.md', 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Old Introduction (lines 9-17 in the file)\nold_intro_start = content.find(\"## Introduction\\n\\n\")\nold_intro_end = content.find(\"\\n---\\n\\n## Methods\", old_intro_start)\nold_intro = content[old_intro_start:old_intro_end]\n\nnew_intro = \"\"\"## Introduction\n\nComputational chemistry has entered an era of abundance. A single project can generate hundreds of docking runs, dozens of molecular dynamics trajectories, and thousands of quantum chemical calculations. Yet the *knowledge* produced by this abundance \u2014 which restraint strategy worked, which scoring function failed, which protocol produced reproducible results \u2014 remains stubbornly transient. Protocol insights live in lab notebooks that no one rereads. Error resolutions are buried in Slack messages and terminal scrollback. Negative findings, arguably the most valuable for preventing wasted effort, are systematically lost because no venue rewards reporting them. When a new graduate student joins a project or a lab moves to a new protein target, the accumulated wisdom of prior work is effectively invisible: it must be rediscovered through the same trial and error that produced it.\n\nThis fragmentation is increasingly at odds with the trajectory of the field. Self-driving laboratories now autonomously design, execute, and analyze experiments for chemistry and materials discovery [1], multi-agent LLM systems decompose complex workflows across specialized agents for chemical engineering [2], and agentic frameworks for computational chemistry translate natural-language intent into validated atomistic simulations [3]. These systems produce data and decisions at a rate that outstrips any individual researcher's ability to track what was tried, what worked, and what failed. The bottleneck is no longer throughput \u2014 it is *retention*. Without a durable memory of protocol outcomes, error resolutions, and negative findings, each generation of automated experiments starts from scratch, rediscovering what prior runs already learned.\n\nRecent work on agent memory has begun to address the retention problem in general-purpose AI systems. MemGPT introduced operating-system-inspired memory management for LLMs, using virtual context to extend effective memory beyond fixed context windows [4]. Generative Agents demonstrated that long-term memory architectures \u2014 combining retrieval, reflection, and planning \u2014 can produce believable agent behavior over extended timescales [5]. Surveys of memory mechanisms for LLM-based agents catalog a growing taxonomy of approaches, including episodic memory, semantic memory, and retrieval-augmented generation, and argue that persistent memory is the missing piece for long-term autonomous agents [6,7,8]. However, these memory architectures have been designed for conversational continuity, task planning, and simulated social behavior. They have not been applied to the specific challenge of accumulating and reusing computational chemistry protocol knowledge across projects.\n\nWorkflow managers and electronic lab notebooks address adjacent aspects of the same problem. Tools like Snakemake, Nextflow, and FireWorks capture *what* was run \u2014 the command, the parameters, the exit code. Electronic lab notebooks record *what* was observed \u2014 a score, a plot, a conclusion. However, these approaches share a fundamental limitation: they treat each session as an independent event. The workflow manager that logged a failure does not prevent the same failure from recurring. The agent that helped dock a peptide on Tuesday has no memory of what it learned on Monday. What is missing is a system that *accumulates* protocol knowledge across sessions and projects, compressing experiences into durable rules that actively shape future behavior \u2014 a memory architecture purpose-built for the error-resolution, parameter-tuning, and protocol-validation cycles that define computational chemistry practice.\n\nWe present Magnolia, an AI agent framework for computational chemistry built around a persistent 3-tier memory architecture. Tier 1 \u2014 the *session tier* \u2014 automatically captures tool invocations, exit codes, and error traces, then distills the conversational context into structured learnings via LLM-driven extraction. Tier 2 \u2014 the *project tier* \u2014 holds confirmed entries (error resolutions, success patterns, failure modes, parameter guidance) that are promoted from staging through a confidence-gated confirmation step. Tier 3 \u2014 the *skill tier* \u2014 elevates project-level learnings into cross-project rules that are loaded into the agent's context at session start and govern subsequent behavior. Rules can encode anything from protocol checklists (validate all input PDBs before submission) to tool-specific pitfalls (template-built peptide geometry produces garbage bond lengths) to scoring conventions (use AIR-free scores, not raw HADDOCK scores, for cross-variant comparison). The promotion from project tier to skill tier is human-gated by design: an LLM proposes candidate elevations based on observation frequency and cross-project corroboration, but only a human confirms them as rules. Critically, Magnolia's role throughout is evidential rather than prescriptive \u2014 it organizes and surfaces results (contact maps, scoring tables, reproducibility metrics) so that the human researcher can see patterns and make decisions. The system amplifies the researcher's ability to learn from their own data; it does not replace their judgment.\n\nAcross a sustained protein\u2013peptide docking campaign against the Hsc70 chaperone \u2014 spanning initial benchmarking, systematic extension, point-mutation scanning, and protocol validation \u2014 plus supplementary projects in ligand screening (Gnina against odorant binding proteins), pocket prediction (P2Rank), and quantum chemistry (xTB), Magnolia accumulated 39 structured project-tier learnings from over 150 tracked runs. The system's evidential role proved decisive at multiple points. Early docking runs using manual subdomain restraints produced erratic, positive scores; Magnolia's structured presentation of per-cluster metrics and contact maps across variants made the pattern visible, prompting the researcher to test P2Rank-guided pocket restraints \u2014 a switch that produced scores from \u221292 to \u221295 AIR-free and enabled the subsequent extension campaign. Later, when blind docking scores for identical peptide sequences varied by over 26 points depending on input conformation, Magnolia's side-by-side scoring tables and contact analyses across protocols revealed that the cmrest workflow was introducing systematic input-geometry bias; the researcher switched to surface-derived restraints, achieving \u03c3 \u2248 5 reproducibility across 15 runs. We also document systematic negative findings that the framework preserved rather than buried: a 42-compound Gnina and HADDOCK3 screen against a lipocalin odorant binding protein showed no correlation with experimental binding affinities (Spearman \u03c1 = \u22120.05 and \u22120.27, respectively) \u2014 a result that, without persistent memory, would likely have been discarded as a failed experiment rather than recorded as a community-relevant finding. We identify the human-gated project-to-rule promotion step as both a necessary quality control and the current throughput bottleneck, and present a self-reflex consolidation pass \u2014 where an LLM proposes merges of duplicate learnings, a human reviews and confirms, and git provides reversibility \u2014 that extends this gated approach from entry creation to entry consolidation, with automated rule-elevation proposals as the remaining step.\"\"\"\n\ncontent = content.replace(old_intro, new_intro)\n\nwith open('/home/tjiang/repos/project_magnolia/opencode_cc_mem/projects/communication/paper/draft_v1.md', 'w') as f:\n    f.write(content)\n\nprint(\"Done. New Introduction written.\")\nprint(f\"Old length: {len(old_intro)}, New length: {len(new_intro)}\")\nPYEOF"}
